{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t01-018",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "memory spans allocation garbage collection pooling and diagnostics",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "Managed objects live in garbage-collected memory and remain alive while reachable from roots. The runtime reclaims unreachable objects automatically; developers still own deterministic disposal of files, sockets, handles, and other non-memory resources.",
    "The generational collector optimizes for short-lived allocations. Frequent small temporary objects can be cheap, while high allocation volume, long-lived graphs, large arrays, pinning, and finalizers can increase collection cost and working set.",
    "`Span<T>` and `ReadOnlySpan<T>` are stack-only views over contiguous memory; `Memory<T>` and `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` can cross async and heap boundaries. These types expose slices without copying, but they do not themselves own or extend the lifetime of the underlying storage.",
    "Pooling can reduce repeated allocation, but rented buffers have explicit ownership and may contain old data. Return each buffer exactly once, stop using it after return, and clear sensitive contents when required.",
    "Optimize memory only after measuring allocation rate, retained heap, GC pause behavior, and end-to-end latency. Use runtime diagnostics and representative workloads; do not call `GC.Collect` as a routine performance fix."
  ],
